  <h2>2. The Service</h2>
  <p>2.1 NeedleBase helps a Business manage customers, measurements, sewing orders (from new order through cutting, sewing, fitting and readiness to collection) and records of payments made to the Business.</p>
  <p>2.2 The Service is <strong>offline-first</strong>. The mobile app can store Business Data on the device in a local database (SQLite). That on-device copy is <strong>not encrypted at rest by the app</strong>. Data is synced to our API and hosted database when an internet connection is available. Syncing may be delayed by connectivity, device or service problems; the Business should check important orders and payments after reconnecting, protect phones with a screen lock, and keep its own backups of anything that has not yet synced. A lost, stolen or reset phone may still hold unsynced records that exist only on that device.</p>
  <p>2.3 We will use reasonable efforts to keep the Service available and secure, but we do not guarantee uninterrupted or error-free availability. Features may change as the Service develops, and some planned features (including any export tool) are not promised unless we state in writing that they are available.</p>
  <p>2.4 Electronic acceptance: an agreement may be formed electronically. The Electronic Transactions Act, 2008 (Act 772) recognises electronic records and agreements (including sections 5 to 8 on electronic records and retention, section 17 on automated transactions and section 23 on formation and validity of agreements). By ticking the acceptance box and creating an account you agree to these Terms and to the Privacy Policy; please save or print a copy for your records (see Act 772, section 47 on information to be provided in electronic transactions, where it applies).</p>
  <h2>3. Eligibility and accounts</h2>
  <p>3.1 You must be 18 years or older and have authority to act for the Business you register. The Owner is responsible for the accuracy of the Business information supplied.</p>
  <p>3.2 The Owner controls Staff accounts and roles. Owners and Managers must only give access to people who need it, must keep passwords private, and must remove access promptly when a person leaves the Business. Tailor accounts, as designed, have narrower access; role settings do not replace the Business's own supervision.</p>
  <p>3.3 You are responsible for activity under your Business's accounts, and must notify us promptly at helmutessien@gmail.com or on WhatsApp +233 50 078 5153 if you suspect unauthorised access.</p>
  <h2>4. Fees, sample prices and licence keys</h2>
  <p>4.1 There is <strong>no in-app billing</strong> in the current product. NeedleBase does not take card, mobile money or bank payments for a subscription inside the app or the website.</p>
  <p>4.2 The public website may show <strong>sample plan prices</strong> (for example monthly and yearly figures in Ghana cedis). Those figures are illustrations only until we confirm them to you in writing. They are not an offer, and they are not a price promised in these Terms.</p>
  <p>4.3 Access to the Service for a Business is granted with a <strong>licence key</strong>. New shops must normally enter a licence key when they register. Keys are agreed and issued <strong>off the app</strong>, after you contact us by WhatsApp or email, and are then entered in NeedleBase. The server stores the key in a protected (encrypted) form and may check the key's status with NeedleBase's licensing service. A key may have a plan, a trial period, a complimentary period, or an expiry date. If a key is no longer valid, the Business may have a short grace period (currently seven days after the licensing service first says the key is not valid) before access is limited.</p>
  <p>4.4 The fee for a key or plan, and any refund, are <strong>as agreed in writing when the key is issued</strong> (for example in our WhatsApp or email messages with you). These Terms do not add a separate refund percentage or cooling-off period. Nothing in this clause takes away any right that Ghana law says you cannot give up.</p>
  <p>4.5 If we later add paid plans or in-app billing, we will give reasonable prior notice (normally at least 30 days) of the price, what it covers and when it starts. A paid plan will not start for your Business until you accept it. Taxes or charges imposed by law, if they apply, will be shown as required.</p>
  <p>4.6 A Business remains responsible for its own taxes, invoices, receipts and filings with the Ghana Revenue Authority and other authorities. Payment records in NeedleBase are business records; they are not tax invoices unless the Business separately issues a valid invoice.</p>
  <h2>5. Payment records — NeedleBase is not a payment service provider</h2>
  <p>5.1 NeedleBase <strong>records</strong> payments that a Business says it has received — for example cash, mobile money (MoMo) or bank payment, in Ghana cedis (GHS). It does not receive, hold, transfer, settle or guarantee money, does not issue electronic money or payment instruments, and does not process MoMo or bank payments.</p>
  <p>5.2 Under the Payment Systems and Services Act, 2019 (Act 987), a body corporate must hold a Bank of Ghana payment system licence before operating as a payment service provider (see section 7). NeedleBase as described in these Terms does not carry on that licensed activity. MoMo providers, banks and other payment providers are third parties with their own terms; disputes about an actual transfer of money are between the Business, its customer and that provider.</p>
  <p>5.3 If NeedleBase ever adds collection, payout or other payment services, those features would require separate terms and, where applicable, Bank of Ghana licensing or authorisation before launch.</p>
  <h2>6. Business Data and ownership</h2>
  <p>6.1 As between the parties, the Business owns its Business Data. You grant HelmutCode Solutions a limited licence to host, copy, transmit, display and process Business Data <strong>only</strong> to provide, secure and maintain the Service, and as otherwise described in the Privacy Policy.</p>
  <p>6.2 You are responsible for the accuracy and lawfulness of Business Data, for having the right to enter customer information, and for keeping your own backups where you need them. Do not enter information the Business has no right to record.</p>
  <h2>7. Data protection (Act 843)</h2>
  <p>7.1 For customer and Staff personal data entered by a Business: the <strong>Business is the data controller</strong> (it decides why and how the data is used) and <strong>HelmutCode Solutions is a data processor / service provider</strong> acting on the Business's instructions. This reflects Act 843, including section 29 (a processor must process only with the controller's knowledge or authorisation and keep data confidential) and section 30 (controller–processor processing should be governed by a written contract with confidentiality and security measures). These Terms, with the Privacy Policy, form that written arrangement for the Service.</p>
  <p>7.2 <strong>Registration.</strong> A data controller who intends to process personal data must register with the Data Protection Commission (Act 843, sections 27, 46 and 53; registration is renewed every two years under section 50). Each Business must assess and, where required, complete its own registration. HelmutCode Solutions is not registered with the Data Protection Commission. A person may complain to the Commission about the handling of their personal data (see <a href="https://dataprotection.org.gh" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">dataprotection.org.gh</a> / <a href="https://dpc.gov.gh" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">dpc.gov.gh</a>).</p>
  <p>7.3 <strong>Safeguards.</strong> Each party will apply appropriate, reasonable technical and organisational measures consistent with Act 843, section 28 (security measures) and section 30 — including access controls by role, hashed passwords and secure sign-in, a protected licence key, and keeping safeguards under review. The copy of Business Data on a phone is not encrypted by the app itself (see clause 2.2).</p>
  <p>7.4 <strong>Security incidents.</strong> Act 843, section 31 requires notification to the Commission and affected data subjects, as soon as reasonably practicable, where there are reasonable grounds to believe personal data has been accessed or acquired by an unauthorised person. If HelmutCode Solutions becomes aware of such an incident affecting Business Data, we will notify the affected Business (normally the Owner) without undue delay and cooperate on investigation and restoration, and the Business (as controller) remains responsible for notifying the Commission and customers where the law requires it. Businesses must promptly pass to us any relevant incident they discover on their own devices. The Cybersecurity Act, 2020 (Act 1038) imposes additional incident duties on owners of designated critical information infrastructure and certain institutions (see sections 39 and 47); NeedleBase is not designated as critical information infrastructure at the date of these Terms, and we will comply with any cybersecurity duty that applies to us.</p>
  <p>7.5 <strong>Data subject requests.</strong> Customers may exercise rights under Act 843 (including access and correction under sections 32, 33 and 35, objection under section 20, and the right to require a controller to stop processing that causes unwarranted damage or distress under section 39). The Business, as controller, must answer those requests; we will provide reasonable assistance (for example by making the relevant records available through the Service or, on a verified Owner request, correcting or deleting records we hold as processor) so the Business can respond in time.</p>
  <p>7.6 <strong>Where data is processed.</strong> Business Data may be stored on the user's device and synced to hosting used for the API and database. The website, API and database are hosted with MonsterASP.NET in Europe, and the public site is at <a href="https://needlebase.runasp.net/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">https://needlebase.runasp.net/</a>. Personal data held on the hosted service is therefore stored in Europe and is transferred outside Ghana. Hosting and other sub-processors are described in the Privacy Policy.</p>
  <h2>8. Acceptable use</h2>
  <p>You must not: use the Service unlawfully or for another person's business without authority; access or attempt to access another Business's data; share accounts in a way that defeats role controls; upload malicious code or disrupt the Service; copy or reverse-engineer the Service except as permitted by law; use customer data for unsolicited direct marketing without the customer's prior written consent (see Act 843, section 40); or enter special personal data (Act 843, section 37 — for example health, religious, political or criminal-record information) that the Business does not lawfully need for tailoring.</p>
